            ],
            "comparisons": [
                {
                    "title": "Classical pipeline vs RL-trained search sub-agent",
                    "left": {
                        "title": "Rewrite -> retrieve -> re-rank",
                        "points": [
                            "Single pass, decisions hard-coded at design time",
                            "Structurally unable to reason over results or do multi-step lookups",
                            "Milliseconds and cheap, but only ~0.45 recall on hard queries",
                            "A re-ranker can flag bad results but cannot search again"
                        ]
                    },
                    "right": {
                        "title": "Agentic retrieval sub-agent (RL-trained)",
                        "points": [
                            "Searches, reads, refines queries over as many steps as needed",
                            "0.84 recall, above GPT-5.1 (0.78) on SID's benchmark",
                            "24x faster than frontier agentic search (5.7s vs 144s)",
                            "Drop-in with existing search tools, no reindexing"
                        ]
                    }
                },
                {
                    "title": "Frontier model doing search vs small specialist doing search",
                    "left": {
                        "title": "Frontier agent searches itself",
                        "points": [
                            "Every rejected document pollutes the main context window",
                            "Minutes of latency on complex retrieval; high per-query cost",
                            "Read/search tool output can dominate the token bill (76.1% in one SWE-bench study)"
                        ]
                    },
                    "right": {
                        "title": "Delegate to a search sub-agent",
                        "points": [
                            "Returns only file paths / doc lists, keeping the parent context clean",
                            "Chroma Context-1: 20B open-weights, up to 10x faster than frontier-scale models",
                            "SWE-grep: 8 parallel tool calls x 4 turns, mini variant at 2,800+ tok/s",
                            "FastContext: up to 60% fewer main-model tokens, up to +5.5% resolution rate"
                        ]
                    }
                }
            ],
            "keyPoints": [
                "Retrieval, not reasoning, is where agent tokens go: Cognition measured agents spending over 60% of their first turn just gathering context.",
                "An independent SWE-bench Verified study found read/search operations consuming 76.1% of all tokens, vs 12.1% execute and 11.8% edit.",
                "Agentic retrieval buys accuracy with tokens: 2.6x token overhead vs single-shot search on BRIGHT, for 5.9x better recall@1.",
                "SID-1 is the first model trained end-to-end with RL purely for agentic retrieval, built on Qwen3-14B with multi-turn, multi-environment RL.",
                "SID's own numbers: 0.84 recall vs 0.78 for GPT-5.1 and 0.45 for classic rerank pipelines, at 24x lower latency (5.7s vs 144s).",
                "The pattern is converging across labs: SID-1, Cognition's SWE-grep and Chroma's Context-1 are all RL-trained retrieval sub-agents sitting next to a frontier model.",
                "Why it works: the sub-agent absorbs the noise, so rejected search results never enter the main agent's context window.",
                "SID is a Y Combinator-backed AI research lab in San Francisco; SID-1 ships via API, AWS Bedrock and self-hosting, currently waitlisted due to limited inference compute.",
                "Caveat: the '100-1000x cost' and '30-50% of tokens' figures come from SID's own talk, not from independent benchmarks - treat as vendor framing.",
                "Note on conflicts: SID's site states 1.9x recall over embedding-only search while its YC page says 1.8x; RL-trained sub-agents still lag pure vector+reranker pipelines on raw latency."
            ],
